- Existing document-related API definitions.
- Existing Event/Request-related API definitions.

3. Investigate Existing Document APIs

Search for existing APIs related to:

- Document Management.
- Fetching documents associated with an event.
- Fetching documents associated with a request.
- Uploading documents.
- Attaching documents to an event/request.
- Removing or deleting documents, if available.

Identify the exact specification files, endpoint paths, HTTP methods and schemas.

Understand how the frontend and backend currently communicate through these contracts.

4. Evaluate the New Removal API

Based on existing conventions, investigate the most appropriate API design for:

- Removing a single document.
- Removing multiple documents.
- Identifying the relevant Event ID / Request ID.
- Passing selected document IDs.
- Returning appropriate success and failure responses.

Do not assume that a DELETE endpoint is necessarily required. Evaluate the existing project conventions first.

5. Permission Investigation

Check whether existing API specifications include permission-related definitions or authorization scopes.

Determine how "removalDocument" should be represented, if applicable.

Do not hardcode permission handling into the API specification unless that matches the existing architecture.

6. Compatibility and Reusability

Before proposing new API definitions:

- Check whether an existing endpoint can be extended.
- Check whether existing request/response schemas can be reused.
- Avoid duplicate endpoints.
- Avoid unnecessary new DTO schemas.
